## 3. Concrete recommendations & best‑practice snippets

> The snippets below can be dropped straight into a new or existing Express project. Where some modules are missing, install them with `npm install <module> --save`.

### 3.1 Use Helmet for secure headers

```js
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');          // <‑ add this
const app = express();

app.use(helmet());                         // <‑ add this
```

*Why?* Helmet sets a plethora of HTTP headers: `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, CSP “report‐only” (you can later enforce it), `Strict-Transport-Security`, etc.

### 3.2 Apply rate limiting

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 1 * 60 * 1000,    // 1 minute
  max: 100,                   // limit each IP to 100 requests per windowMs
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' },
});

app.use(limiter);              // <‑ add this, before your routes
```

*Why?* Helps mitigate DoS attacks – a trivial micro‑service is often a brute‑force target.

### 3.3 Enable CORS only for allowed origins

```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = process.env.CORS_ORIGINS
  ? process.env.CORS_ORIGINS.split(',')
  : ['http://localhost:3000'];   // fall back for dev

app.use(cors({
  origin: allowedOrigins,
  optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
}));
```

### 3.4 Use environment variables

`app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000, …)`

```
# .env (not committed to VCS)
PORT=8080
CORS_ORIGINS=https://example.com
```

### 3.5 Central error handler

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err); // consider rotating logs or sending to Sentry
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
});
```

### 3.6 Setup basic request logging

```js
const morgan = require('morgan');
app.use(morgan('combined'));   // change to 'common' in local dev
```

### 3.7 Guard against “JSON Hijacking”

While Express' default `res.json()` sets `Content-Type: application/json`, the old “JSON Hijacking” vulnerability can still be mitigated by wrapping responses in a defensive prefix:

```js
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.set('X-Content-Type-Options', 'nosniff');
  res.send('while(1);'); // defensive prefix before data if JSONP
  res.json([...]);       // real response
});
```

(If you don't use JSONP or serve JSON only, the extra layer is usually unnecessary.)

### 3.8 Use HTTPS in production

In production, you’ll normally serve Express behind a reverse‑proxy (NGINX, Traefik, etc.) that handles TLS. Ensure you:

```js
app.set('trust proxy', 1); // if behind a proxy
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (req.secure) { return next(); }
  res.redirect(301, `https://${req.headers.host}${req.url}`);
});
```

