## Toward a production‑ready secrets strategy

Instead of a plain `.env` file, you may want to integrate a secrets manager such as:

| Cloud | Secrets Manager | How to use in Node |
|-------|-----------------|--------------------|
| AWS   | AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store | `aws-sdk` or `@aws-sdk/client-secrets-manager`, retrieve at runtime |
| Azure | Azure Key Vault | `@azure/keyvault-secrets` |
| GCP   | Secret Manager | `@google-cloud/secret-manager` |
| Docker/K8s | Docker secrets / Kubernetes secrets | Volume or environment variable injection |

**Typical load pattern (AWS Secrets Manager example)**

```js
const { SecretsManagerClient, GetSecretValueCommand } = require('@aws-sdk/client-secrets-manager');

async function loadSecrets() {
  const client = new SecretsManagerClient({ region: process.env.AWS_REGION });
  const secretName = process.env.SECRET_NAME || 'myNodeAppSecrets';
  const command = new GetSecretValueCommand({ SecretId: secretName });

  try {
    const data = await client.send(command);
    const secretString = data.SecretString ? JSON.parse(data.SecretString) : null;
    if (!secretString) {
      throw new Error('Secrets string missing');
    }
    // Expected shape:
    // { JWT_SECRET: '...', DB_PASSWORD: '...' }
    return secretString;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch secrets:', err);
    process.exit(1);
  }
}

// Call during app bootstrap
(async () => {
  const secrets = await loadSecrets();
  app.locals.jwtSecret = secrets.JWT_SECRET;
  // … use app.locals.jwtSecret in your auth middleware
})();
```

